.Tg imperative_versus_declarative
.It
.Lk ""
An imperative programming language is a language where the computer is
instructed step by step. A declarative programming language, on the other hand,
lets you write code that describes what you want, and the computer figures out
how to reach that state. When we talk about computer systems, imperative means
a system where commands and processes update the internal state. A declarative
system, such as in NixOS, has its state declared as a set of rules and
definitions that the computer automatically evaluates.
.Lk #b14 ↩
